# ScanGrade — the pipeline's way into a box that cannot deploy.
#
# The runner reads this out of the commit it has already fetched, on every tick, and
# obeys it BEFORE it decides anything about a release. That ordering is the point: a
# box whose release is refused, quarantined or paused is exactly the box that cannot be
# reached by pushing a fix, because the thing that would apply the fix is the thing
# that is refusing. This file is read without merging anything, so it arrives on a box
# whose tree is dirty, rolled back or frozen.
#
# Format — one `key: value` per line, `#` lines and blank lines ignored:
#
#   command   none | recover | release | rebaseline | pause | resume
#   issued    when the command was published (UTC, ISO-8601). It is what makes a
#             re-issued command a *new* plan: a plan is obeyed once per exact content,
#             so re-publishing only the same bytes would be obeyed once and then never
#             again. `deploy/scangrade-plan.sh` writes a fresh one every run.
#   note      one line of free text, carried into the record and the journal.
#
# What each command does, and what it deliberately does not:
#
#   recover     runs the box's own recovery lever (`deploy/scangrade-recover.sh`,
#               installed as `sgfix`) and ends the tick — the lever runs a release of
#               its own, so carrying on would deploy twice for one command. It answers
#               a missing migration, a stale perf baseline and a box-local edit.
#   release     writes the same release request the deploy-status page writes, so the
#               quarantine on the currently-held commit is lifted and that commit tried
#               once more.
#   rebaseline  asks the perf gate to re-measure the box (`--rebaseline`) instead of
#               comparing against a baseline that may no longer describe it.
#   pause       freezes deploys (the exam-week freeze), so a freeze can be set from the
#               pipeline rather than from a shell.
#   resume      removes that freeze. This is the one state with no other way out: the
#               runner exits on the pause file, so a command read *after* the pause
#               check could never arrive.
#   none        the no-op. It exists so the channel has a state that means "nothing is
#               being asked" rather than an absent file, which is indistinguishable
#               from a branch the box has not fetched yet.
#
# Unknown commands are refused by name and recorded — never ignored — because a plan
# the reader silently skips is a box nobody can command and nobody can tell is
# uncommanded.
#
# Publish with:
#
#     bash deploy/scangrade-plan.sh recover "why, in one line"
#
# The record of what was obeyed lands on the box at
# /var/lib/scangrade-deploy/control/<plan-hash>.applied, and the same file is what the
# runner reads to know a plan has already been obeyed.
command: none
issued: 2026-09-30T22:00:00Z
note: the channel exists, and is asking for nothing
